\section{Chemistry in \SFt}
\label{sec:chemistry}

Cassirer referred to chemistry throughout \SFt, but devoted section \rom{8} of the fourth chapter, on concept formation \origg{Begriffsbildung} in the natural sciences, specifically to chemical concept formation. Chemistry had initially developed as a purely \s{descriptive science}, much like zoology or botany. However, by the time Cassirer was writing, the emergence of physical chemistry in the late nineteenth century had firmly established chemistry among the \s{constructive sciences} \citep[see][]{Nernst1896}. In [-particular-]{+particular,+} chemists increasingly turned to thermodynamics to investigate chemical processes, analyzing reaction rates and equilibrium conditions in terms of macroscopic variables such as temperature, pressure, and energy. As Cassirer points out, from this point of view, chemistry's \qt{fundamental laws, as for example the law of phases (\emph{Phasenregel}) of Gibbs or the law of chemical masses (\emph{Massenwirkung}), belong to the same purely mathematical type as the propositions of theoretical physics}{Ihre Grundgesetze, wie etwa die Gibbs'sche Phasenregel oder das Gesetz der chemischen Massenwirkung, gehören demselben rein mathematischen Typus an, wie irgendwelche Sätze der theoretischen Physik} \SF{271}{204}\footnoteh{While the \s{phase rule} concerns the coexistence of phases and the \s{law of mass action} specifies the equilibrium relations among reacting species, both receive a unified formulation within the thermodynamics of Gibbs, Helmholtz, Duhem, and Planck through the notion of thermodynamic potential. Alongside this program of determining the conditions of chemical equilibrium, molecular and ionic accounts of chemical phenomena were already being developed, notably by van 't Hoff and Arrhenius, without yet relying on a theory of the internal electronic structure of atoms}. The difference between physics and chemistry thus lies not in their status as \s{exact sciences}, but in the historical paths by which they attained that status. 

Physics had the advantage of dealing with relatively simple systems, such as planetary motion, in which bodies could be treated as \s{structureless} mass points. It could therefore replace the thing-concept \origg{Dingbegriff} with the concept of law \origg{Gesetzesbegriff} at an early stage. Chemistry, by contrast, had to begin with the problem of the individual thing [-\origg{Einzelding} in all its complexity \SF{271}{204}---specific properties if-]{+\origg{Einzelding}, that is, with the specific+} properties of elements and [-compounds.-]{+compounds in all their complexity \SF{271}{204}.+} Cassirer therefore asks whether chemistry requires a different form of concept formation from that of physics. As one might expect, Cassirer argues that this question can be answered only \qt{if we follow the concrete historical development of chemical doctrines themselves}{wenn man dem konkreten geschichtlichen Gang der chemischen Lehren selbst folgt} \SF{272}{204}. Given the systematic aims of the book, Cassirer does not attempt to trace the history of chemistry in detail, but seeks instead to identify \qt{the great logical lines of direction}{die großen logischen Richtlinien} along which it developed \SF{272}{204}. \todo{sources} Cassirer does not appear to draw directly on the original sources, relying instead on \citet{Wurtz1879}, \citet{Meyer1884}, \citet{Berthelot1885}, \citet{Ladenburg1902}, [-\citet{Duhem1902}-]{+\citet{Duhem1902},+} and \citet{Ostwald1906Leitlinien} as his principal historical accounts of the development of chemistry. Cassirer reorganizes this material to fit his own philosophical narrative. 

\subsection{From Definite to Multiple [-Proportion}-]{+Proportions}+}

