\subsection{From Equivalent Weights to Atomic Weights}

In this sense, Cassirer could point [-out,-]{+out+} that the compositional formula has a purely macroscopic quantitative content, [-independently-]{+independent+} of its interpretation in terms of atoms. The goal of the \s{law of constant combining numbers} \origg{Verbindungszahlen} was initially to establish a characteristic equivalent number for each element \origg{Äquivalenzzahl}, such that whenever two or more elements enter into a compound, their masses stand to one another \qt{as whole multiples of these numbers}{wenn je zwei oder mehrere Elemente in eine Verbindung eingehen, ihre Massen sich wie ganze Vielfache  dieser Grundzahlen verhalt} \SF{274}{206}. In principle, these ratios can be established by measuring the macroscopic masses of substances involved in chemical reactions. In Dalton's work, however, this rule of \s{multiple proportions} becomes associated with a particular interpretation: \qt{The concept of combination-weight \origins{Verbindungsgewichts} is transformed into that of atomic weight \origins{Atomgewichts}}{Der Begriff des Verbindungsgewichts wandelt sich zu dem des Atomgewichts} \SF{274}{206}. Although the law does not itself require matter to be discontinuous, the occurrence of simple integer ratios receives a natural explanation if chemical elements consist of discrete units with fixed masses. Dalton interpreted such regularities by assuming that substances consist of indivisible atoms, each element possessing atoms of a characteristic mass, with hydrogen $= 1$ as the unit standard. Different elements possess atoms of different masses, while all atoms of the same element possess the same constant mass: \qt{In place of the empirically gained proportion-numbers \origg{Proportionalzahlen} of the individual bodies, assertions appear regarding an essential property of their ultimate constitutive parts}{An die Stelle  der empirisch ermittelten Proportionalzahlen der einzelnen Körper sind Aussagen über eine  wesentliche Beschaffenheit ihrer le