To construct a systematic account of chemical substitution, chemists needed a common unit for comparing the combining capacities of different elements. Just as hydrogen served as the conventional standard for relative atomic weights, its combining capacity could serve as the unit of valence. Within the classical valence scheme, chlorine, oxygen, nitrogen, and carbon were assigned combining capacities of one, two, three, and four, respectively: $\mathrm{Cl}\sim 1\mathrm{H}$, $\mathrm{O}\sim 2\mathrm{H}$, $\mathrm{N}\sim 3\mathrm{H}$, and $\mathrm{C}\sim 4\mathrm{H}$. This establishes a further basis for relating the individual elements to one another, introducing a \qt{new characteristic constant}{neue charakteristische Konstante} for each elementary substance: \qt{The \s{valency} of the elements is the expression of a definite property in them, that belongs to them independently of their chemical affinity}{Die ,,Valenz" der Elemente ist der Ausdruck einer bestimmten Grund- beschaffenheit in ihnen, die ihnen unabhängig von ihrer chemischen Verwandtschaft zukommt} \SF{281}{211}. The concept of valence arises from the demand for consistency.  Without [-the concept of valence,-]{+it,+} one might merely record individual cases in which one element [-replace-]{+replaces+} another in compounds. Valence brings them under [-a-]{+the+} general rule that substitution must preserve the total valence, allowing one to determine a coherent system of substitution relations across different [-compounds-]{+compounds.+}

