At the time Cassirer wrote this letter, the second edition of the first volume of \EPt had already been published, but Cassirer promised to [-taken into account-]{+take+} Meyerson's comments [-for-]{+into account in+} the second volume. After visiting Meyerson in Paris at the end of March 1911 \letterecndp{Cassirer}{Meyerson}{30}{3}{1911}[164], Cassirer wrote to him again just after the [-\EPt:-]{+second volume of \EPt appeared:+} \qt{You will notice for yourself that I have made extensive use of your review, and that it has been extremely valuable to me: for example, I have now added a new section on the development of chemistry\footnote{\cite[2:428--441]{Cassirer1911a}}}{Sie erhalten heute den II. Band des Erkenntnisproblems in zweiter Auflage. Ich würde mich sehr freuen, wenn Sie gelegentlich Zeit fänden, ihn durchzusehen und mir über ihn Ihre Meinung zu sagen. Daß ich Ihre Recension vielfach benutzt habe und daß sie mir gute Dienste geleistet hat, werden Sie selbst bemerken: so ist z. B. jetzt ein Abschnitt über die Entwickelung der Chemie neu hinzugekommen und auch manche Einzelheit, auf die Sie hingewiesen haben, berücksichtigt worden.} \letterecndp{Cassirer}{Meyerson}{25}{10}{1911}[178]. In the new section, devoted entirely to the problem of matter in chemistry, Cassirer makes a concerted effort to atone for his \s{sin of omission} by addressing the historical dimension of Meyerson's objections. At the same time, however, he skillfully integrates these historical details into his own conceptual framework.

[-The-]{+Cassirer now discusses the+} role {+of+} Boyle's corpuscularism in the history of [-chemistry is introduced,-]{+chemistry,+} but [-to show that-]{+presents it+} as the first step in a process in which nature is stripped of its \qt{inner qualitative strivings and forces}, and Boyle conceives of nature not as a separate agent but as a \q{system of rules} prescribing how [-corpuscle-]{+corpuscles+} act upon[-one another-] and are acted upon [-each other-]{+by one another+} \citep[2:433]{Cassirer1911a}. [-Still-]{+Still,+} Cassirer suggests that {+the+} influence of this view on chemistry proper was initially meager\hide{Der neue Gedanke, der hier ausgesprochen ist, findet jedoch im speziellen Ausbau der Chemie selbst nur sehr allmählich Verständnis und Anerkennung}. John \citet{Freind1709} sought to reconstruct the entire discipline along Newtonian lines, introducing the concept of attraction as an explanatory principle\hide{In den »Praelectiones chymicae« vom Jahre 1709 versucht Freind die gesamte Chemie nach Newtonischer Methode abzuleiten, indem er den Begriff der Attraktion in sie einzuführen und für die Erklärung fruchtbar zu machen sucht}. Such attempts, however, ultimately remained sterile, since they were still insufficiently supported by experience\hide{Aber dieses Bemühen um eine chemische Dynamik blieb vereinzelt, da der Stand der empirischen Tatsachenkenntnis ihm nirgends entsprach} \citep[2:434--435]{Cassirer1911a}. The first half of the eighteenth century remained dominated by the phlogiston theory, in which \qt{the qualitative conception of natural processes once again comes characteristically to the fore}{die qualitative Auffassung des Naturgeschehens noch einmal in bezeichnender Weise hervortritt} \citep[2:434]{Cassirer1911a}.

% 
%E.g. Lavoisier introduced a purely \s{quantitative} distinction between \s{element} and \s{compound}: compounds can be split into products that each weigh less than the starting material, elements cannot \citep[see][22--23]{Ostwald1906Leitlinien}. 

Cassirer now acknowledges that \qt{only with Lavoisier's reform \textelp{} is the scientific concept of chemistry itself transformed}{Erst Lavoisiers Reform führt eine prinzipiell neue Betrachtungsweise ein, in welcher sich der wissenschaftliche Begriff der Chemie selbst umgestaltet} \citep[2:435]{Cassirer1911a}. Whereas \SFt mentions Lavoisier only in passing, the second edition of \EPt places him at the center of the narrative. While the phlogistonists treated quantitative measurements as secondary, Lavoisier made the quantitative relations between the initial and final states of chemical transformations the criterion for identifying a \s{substance}. Once again, however, Cassirer makes this case study work in his favor. Whereas Meyerson emphasized the continuity of explanation in terms of persistent constituents across the transition from phlogiston theory to Lavoisier's chemistry, Cassirer interpreted the same episode as a functional reinterpretation of the concept of substance: \qt{for here the concept of substance passes definitively from its ontological into its modern epistemological-critical meaning, being conceived as a pure correlative concept to the idea of quantitative conservation}{Dennoch bedeutet Lavoisiers Leistung implizit einen der wichtigsten Schritte in der Richtung auf die Erkenntnis der logischen Grundsätze der Erfahrungserkenntnis selbst: Denn der Begriff der Substanz geht hier endgültig von seiner ontologischen in seine moderne erkenntniskritische Bedeutung über, indem er als reiner Korrelatbegriff zum Gedanken der quantitativen Erhaltung gefaßt wird} \citep[2:440]{Cassirer1911a}.

The [-Meyerson–Cassirer-]{+Meyerson--Cassirer+} \s{battle over the substance-concept} was fought with historical weapons, but the battlefield was ultimately a purely systematic one. Cassirer was ready to accommodate Meyerson’s objections on historical grounds, though he entirely rejected their systematic implications. Meyerson contends that scientists continuously appeal to \s{things} that [-remains-]{+remain+} constant [-beyond-]{+beneath+} the apparent variety of[-the-] phenomena (Boyle treats his [-corpuscle-]{+corpuscles as being+} as real as tables and chairs). By contrast, Cassirer could easily reinterpret the same episode as confirming his narrative of the progressive reduction of the substance-concept to the function-concept (Boyle's [-corpuscle moves-]{+corpuscles move+} according to mathematical laws). For all their differences, Meyerson found the second edition of \EPt \qt{considerably improved}{ganz wesentlich verbessert} \letterecndp{Meyerson}{Cassirer}{15}{11}{1911}[180]. Eager to see his work gain recognition, Meyerson likely appreciated that Cassirer, a rising figure in German philosophy, had taken his suggestions seriously, even if he incorporated them into a very different narrative. If the [-Meyerson–Cassirer-]{+Meyerson--Cassirer+} debate teaches us anything, it is perhaps the limitations of such {+a+} \french{grand récit}: [-Both-]{+both+} Meyerson's and Cassirer's narratives turn out to be at once too narrow to accommodate the full range of historical evidence and so broad that virtually any case can be made to fit them.

