Because chemical experiments could only measure \emph{relative} atomic weights, some [-19th-century-]{+nineteenth-century+} chemists could treat atoms as a useful mathematical fiction. [-Perrin using-]{+Using+} independent measurements (Brownian motion, electrical measurements, and [-radioactivity)-]{+radioactivity), Perrin+} was able to determine Avogadro's constant \citep[281]{Perrin1910}, providing evidence for the existence of atoms [-of-]{+with determinate+} \emph{absolute} weights in grams. During the [-discussion-]{+discussion,+} Meyerson [-could-]{+did+} indeed turn this result to [-its-]{+his+} \s{own advantage}. Although physicists might {+often+} be[-ofnte-] \s{professed positivists}, they were invariably \s{practicing realists} [-that cannot-]{+who could not+} resist treating atoms as \s{real} entities. By sending the paper to [-Cassirer-]{+Cassirer,+} Meyerson wanted to \s{score a point} in their ongoing debate. Cassirer’s response to Meyerson’s letter has a cordial yet detached tone \letterecndp{Cassirer}{Meyerson}{23}{6}{1913}[18][203]. Indeed, he might have felt that Meyerson's objection rested on a fundamental misunderstanding of the issue at stake.

If we consider Cassirer’s relatively sparse remarks on the subject in his epistemological writings over the following decade, it becomes clear that his concern was not with the existence of \s{atoms}, a question he left to science [-do-]{+to+} decide, but rather with the nature of the scientific \s{atom-concept} \origg{Atombegriff}. By following the history of the atom-concept, according to Cassirer, one can see how the constancy of the atom as a thing \origg{Dingbegriff} gives way to the constancy of a numerical ratio \origg{Maßbegriff}: \qt{fundamentally, such a realization, such a strictly quantitative meaning of the concept of the atom, was only reached in the beginnings of modern chemistry in the law of multiple proportion}{Im Grunde wird eine solche Erfüllung, wird ein streng exakter quantitativer Sinn des Atombegriffs erst mit den Anfängen der modernen Chemie im Gesetz der multiplen Proportionen erreicht} \ER{17}{360}. As we have seen, chemistry progressively determined \emph{relative} atomic weights through the convergence of independent lines of evidence \hide{(the Dulong–Petit law, the work of Avogadro and Cannizzaro, \etc)}; Perrin's work subsequently enabled the unambiguous determination of the \emph{absolute} mass of an individual atom through the convergence of independent [-measurement.-]{+measurements.+}  For Cassirer, the logical significance of these measurements lies not in the proof of the existence of atoms, but in the fact that the assumption of the existence of atoms leads to the determination of a universal constant. The fact that heterogeneous phenomena can be coordinated through a single numerical constant provides the only [-warranty-]{+warrant+} for the \emph{objectivity} of physical knowledge, {+understood+} as {+the+} progressive [-\s{de-anthropormophiszation}-]{+\s{de-anthropomorphization}+} of nature: \qt{Physical thought is always concerned at first with establishing a characteristic[-a characteristic-] point of view of measurement in an objective physical concept, in a determinate constant of nature}{Immer handelt es sich für die physikalische Auffassung darum, zunächst einen charakteristischen Gesichtspunkt der Messung in einem objektiv-physikalischen Begriff, in einer bestimmten Naturkonstante, zu fixieren} [-\ER{17}{359\tm}-]{+\ER{17}{359\tm}.+}

Cassirer appropriated this \s{Planckian} [-move of-]{+move,+} replacing \s{realism} about theoretical entities with[-\s{objectivity} of-] the [-measurement-]{+\s{objectivity}+} of {+measured+} numerical [-constants-]{+constants,+} in various forms throughout his subsequent writings \PSF{520}{446}\footnoteh{E.g.: \q{Der moderne Physiker vermag auf die Frage, worin das eigentliche »Objektive« der Natur besteht, keine andere Antwort zu geben, als daß er die »universellen Konstanten« namhaft macht, bei deren Feststellung seine Forschung endet, und daß er andererseits den Weg von diesen universellen Konstanten bis zu den Individualkonstanten, zu den besonderen Dingkonstanten, hinab verfolgt}}. However, it was only in \citets{Cassirer1936} later monograph on the new quantum theory that {+he found+} the opportunity to [-reorganize-]{+organize+} these remarks [-in-]{+into a+} coherent [-form arose.-]{+form.+} The question of the reality of atoms has always led to the opposition between \s{positivism} and \s{realism}. However, this is a false dichotomy. Those who regard atoms as \s{fictions} cannot account for the extraordinary heuristic power of the atomic hypothesis; those who regard them as \s{things} fail to account for the constant change of atomic images. This dialectic can be overcome once one recognizes {+that+} the ultimate value of the atomic hypothesis lies in its \german{Sp\"urkraft}, its capacity to uncover previously unknown constants of nature. [-E.g-]{+For example, the+} kinetic[-gas-] theory {+of gases+} leads to[-determine-] the {+determination of+} Loschmidt's constant: \qt{These are new universal constants to whose knowledge the atomic hypothesis leads—knowledge possessing the \myemph{highest degree of \s{objectivity} that natural science can possibly achieve}}{Es sind somit neue universelle Konstanten, zu deren Kenntnis die atomistische Hypothese hinführt – eine Kenntnis, die das höchste Ausmaß an »Objektivität« besitzt, das innerhalb der Naturwissenschaft überhaupt erreichbar ist} \DI{178}{143\tm}. The model-like representations of the atom may change while the universal quantitative relations they establish survive [-\DI{179}{144}..-]{+\DI{179}{144}.+} 

Once [-again-]{+again,+} Cassirer resorts to a [-long-]{+lengthy+} historical reconstruction of the development of the \s{atom-concept} to make his point. \s{Physical atoms} were initially introduced speculatively as[-atoms as-] small, [-impenetrable-]{+impenetrable,+} \s{hard} bodies. However, as \citet{Lasswitz1890} showed, Huygens introduced atoms as a consequence of the conservation of energy and momentum. While Boyle[-initially-] conceived {+of+} \s{chemical [-atom}-]{+atoms}+} as thing-like entities, Dalton [-presents-]{+presented+} them as [-the byproduct-]{+a by-product+} of the empirically confirmed law of multiple proportions \DI{183}{147}:

\qt{As with Huygens, the atom is here defined strictly substantially; but all statements concerning its nature \origins{Was} ultimately merge into statements concerning quantity-definite numerical relations \origins{Wieviel}. All the basic concepts and theories of chemistry, the concepts of valence and types, the concept of the radical as well as the theory of compound radicals all point in this direction. The conceptual language of atomism in conjunction with the general symbolic language of mathematics now permits that simple and magnificent development in the language of chemistry that is evident in the progress from simple to structural formulas. Stereochemistry, as established by van 't Hoff, permitted the elucidation, through a complex construction of formulas, of an entirely new group of facts, the phenomena of stereoisomerism; it threw new light on the distribution of atoms in space. Thus the visual picture of the atom is modified and refined, but all these changes serve only the one purpose of circumscribing and representing an increasingly large domain of functional relations}{Wie bei Huyghens, so wird hier das Atom streng substantiell gefaßt; aber alle Aussagen über sein »Was« gehen zuletzt in Aussagen über ein »Wieviel«, in Aussagen über bestimmte numerische Verhältnisse über. Alle Grundbegriffe und alle Theorien der wissenschaftlichen Chemie – der Valenzbegriff und der Typenbegriff wie der Begriff des Radikals und die Theorie der zusammengesetzten Radikale – weisen in diese Richtung. Die Begriffssprache der Atomistik erlaubt jetzt, in Gemeinschaft mit der allgemeinen Zeichensprache der Mathematik, jenen einfachen und großartigen Aufbau der chemischen Formelsprache, wie er sich im Fortgang von den einfachen Formeln zu den Strukturformeln vollzieht. Die Stereochemie, wie sie von van’t Hoff begründet wird, erlaubt auf Grund einer komplexeren Gestalt der Formeln eine neue Gruppe von Tatsachen, insbesondere die Erscheinungen der Isomerie, zum Ausdruck zu bringen; sie gibt einen neuen Aufschluß über die Lagerung der Atome im Raume. So wandelt und verfeinert sich das anschauliche Bild des Atoms; aber alle diese Wandlungen dienen nur dem einen Zweck, einen immer größeren Kreis funktioneller Beziehungen zu umspannen und darzustellen}[\DI{183--84}{147}]
%
The discovery of the electron brought together the previously separate histories of the chemical and {+the+} physical atom. The indivisible chemical atom turned into a composite system of electrons (Thomson's \s{plum pudding} model) [-and-]{+and, after+} the discovery of the [-nucleus fixed-]{+nucleus,+} into a spatially structured system (Bohr's planetary model) \DI{184--186}{148--150}. From this point of view, as Cassirer put it, it is hard to avoid the impression that the history of atomic theories [-appears to deliver-]{+offers+} a \q{kaleidoscopic} variety, a strange \q{phantasmagoria} of disconnected portraits \DI{186}{149}. On closer inspection, however, each theory identifies and [-correct-]{+corrects+} the errors of its predecessor while advancing \qt{in a single direction}{eine einheitliche \ls{Richtung}} \DI{187}{150}. What {+matters is not what+} the atom ultimately \s{is}, but[-resolves-] the historical stability of {+the+} universal constants that it allows {+us+} to uncover: \qt{As long as this empirical interconnection is safeguarded, and as long as we are convinced that the progression of \s{atomic models} is guided not by mood or caprice but by a definite rule, the atom has all the \s{reality} of which it is capable}{So brauchen wir denn auf die Frage, was das Atom oder Elektron denn nun eigentlich und endgültig sei, keine Antwort zu finden, wenn wir nur sicher sind, daß die verschiedenen Formen seiner Bestimmung, die uns die fortschreitende experimentelle und theoretische Forschung in die Hand gibt, ineinander eingreifen und sich wechselseitig ergänzen. Ist dieser empirische Zusammenhang gesichert, können wir uns überzeugt halten, daß im Fortgang der »Atommodelle« nicht Laune oder Willkür, sondern eine bestimmte Regel herrscht, so hat das Atom damit all jene »Realität«, deren es fähig ist} \DI{187}{150}. 


